Egan locates the seeds of reluctance in the client. Managing problems and developing unused opportunities is hard work, and the payoffs are not always immediately evident. Karl, the ex-serviceman with PTSD symptoms, knows he needs to get back into community and talks with Laura about steps such as reconnecting with his local church, yet he is slow to act. He "talks the talk" but keeps finding reasons not to "walk the walk".

Early form: avoiding help. Being slow to seek or accept help is an early form of reluctance. Vogel, Wester and Larson (2007) outline why troubled people avoid helping at all. Common beliefs include: society looks down on help-seekers (and some people do); the experience will be too emotionally painful; counselling probably will not help much; I will have to reveal all my dark secrets; family and friends will think me odd; and I will be embarrassed and feel worse about myself.

Definition. For those who do see a helper, reluctance refers to their hesitancy to engage in the work demanded by the tasks of the helping process. Because problem management and opportunity development involve so much work, sources of reluctance exist in all clients, indeed in all human beings. Saving a failing marriage takes great effort; conquering an addiction is hard, and some people "give up" smoking dozens of times before succeeding. Unused opportunities also bring reluctance, because developing them means venturing into unknown waters, which some find charming and others terrifying. Socially shy clients may choose a lonely life over small steps toward real friendship; one client with dozens of Facebook "friends" she never met became deeply depressed over her "make-believe" social life and called herself an "Internet fraud".

Forms of reluctance (often covert). Reluctant clients:
- talk only about safe or low-priority issues;
- seem unsure of what they want;
- benignly sabotage the process by being overly cooperative;
- set unrealistic goals and then use them as an excuse for not moving forward;
- do not work very hard at changing their behaviour;
- are slow to take responsibility for themselves;
- blame others or the social settings and systems of their lives;
- play games with helpers;
- or do not come for counselling in the first place.

Tim, for example, is reluctant to join his wife's counselling sessions; he will "think about it", feels no "real need", and cannot "find the time" because of work, but deep down he is afraid of what might happen. Egan concludes that reluctance to change is normal, that we need only reflect on our own experience to see it, and that it admits of degrees: clients come "armored" against change to a greater or lesser extent.

Involuntary clients (Brodsky, 2011), sometimes called "mandated" clients, are often resisters. Examples: a high school student sent to a counsellor after trouble with a teacher sees it as punishment; a felon gets probation on condition of counselling; a manager accused of sexual harassment keeps his job only if he attends sessions. Such clients appear in schools (especially below college level), correctional settings, marriage counselling (especially court-mandated), employment and welfare agencies, court-related settings and other social agencies. But any client who feels coerced or unfairly treated can become a resister.

Egan lists kinds of clients who are often resistant:
- those who see no reason for seeing a helper at all;
- those who resent third-party referrers (parents, teachers, correctional facilities, social service agencies), with the resentment carrying over to the helper;
- those who do not know what helping is about and fear the unknown;
- those with a history of rebelliousness;
- those who see the helper's or the system's goals as different from their own (e.g., a welfare setting aiming at financial independence while the client is content with dependency);
- those with negative attitudes and suspicions about helping and helpers, who do not trust "shrinks";
- those who equate seeing a helper with admitting weakness, failure and inadequacy and resist to save face and preserve self-esteem;
- those who feel counselling is being done to them and their rights are not respected;
- those who need personal power and find it by resisting a powerful figure or agency ("I may be relatively powerless, but I still have the power to resist");
- those who dislike their helpers but do not discuss it;
- those who differ from their helpers about how much change is needed;
- those who differ greatly from their helpers, such as a poor kid with an older middle-class helper.

Kiracofe and Wells (2007) object to mandated or disciplinary counselling in educational institutions on professional and ethical grounds. They argue that it muddies self-responsibility, because the mandatory referral implicitly moves responsibility for behaviour change from the student to the counselling process. They propose strategies for judicial action based on students' readiness for change, aligned with Prochaska's stages. Egan agrees misconduct will not go away, that teachers and administrators have tended to pass the buck, and that the article calls for professional debate and a systemic solution.

Sociocultural variables such as gender, prejudice, race, religion, social class, upbringing and cultural or subcultural blueprints can also feed resistance. A man may instinctively resist help from a woman and vice versa; an African American may resist help from a white person and vice versa; a person with no religious affiliation may assume help from a minister will be "pious" or involve proselytizing. Ultimately it is the helper's job to spot resistance and work with the client to find its cause.

Because reluctance and resistance are so pervasive, helping clients manage them is part of all interactions with clients (Kottler, 1992). The first principle concerns what not to do.

Helpers, especially beginners unaware of how common these phenomena are, are often thrown when they meet uncooperative clients. They may feel confusion, panic, irritation, hostility, guilt, hurt, rejection or depression. Distracted by these feelings, they respond unhelpfully. They may:
- accept their guilt and try to placate the client;
- become impatient and hostile, verbally or nonverbally;
- do nothing, hoping the reluctance or resistance will disappear;
- lower their expectations of themselves and continue half-heartedly;
- become warmer and more accepting, trying to win the client over by love;
- blame the client and end up in a power struggle;
- let themselves be abused, playing scapegoat;
- lower their expectations of what counselling can achieve;
- hand the direction of the process over to the client;
- give up.

In short, "difficult" clients cause stress, and some helpers give in to self-defeating "fight or flight" ways of handling it.

The stress comes not only from client behaviour but from the helper's own self-defeating attitudes and assumptions, which are never spoken but loiter in the background:
- all clients should be self-referred and committed to change before arriving;
- every client must like and trust me;
- I am a consultant, not a social influencer, so I should not place demands on clients or help them place demands on themselves;
- every unwilling client can be helped;
- no unwilling client can be helped;
- I alone am responsible for what happens to this client;
- I must succeed completely with every client.

Effective helpers neither court reluctance and resistance nor are surprised by them.
